﻿@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Branch by abstraction";
}

<section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Timesheet &middot; how it was refactored</p>
        <h1>Branch by abstraction</h1>
        <p class="lead">How one MVC app became a shared core with three front ends, without breaking it along the way.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<main class="wrap grid">
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="steps-title">
        <h2 id="steps-title">The steps</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>The original project was MVC Classic, with the domain layer in the same project as the MVC UI. Unit tests for the domain layer were in a separate test project.</li>
            <li><b>Introduce the abstraction.</b> The domain layer moved into its own project behind a clear interface. The unit tests showed it still behaved the same.</li>
            <li><b>Point the existing UI at it.</b> The MVC Classic UI was changed to depend on the new domain project.</li>
            <li><b>Reuse it for more UIs:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li>a hybrid UI (MVC/Razor Pages hosting Blazor components);</li>
                    <li>a Blazor Server UI.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="projects-title">
        <h2 id="projects-title">Projects before and after</h2>
        <div class="figure">
            <img src="~/images/abstraction-of-projects.png" alt="Solution Explorer before: Timesheet (domain layer and UI) and Timesheet.Tests. After: Timesheet.Blazor.Server (UI), Timesheet.Core (domain layer), Timesheet.Mvc.Classic (UI), Timesheet.Mvc.RazorComponents (UI) and Timesheet.Tests" />
        </div>
    </section>

    <partial name="_Variants" model="@("Hybrid")" />
</main>
